          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Tank Systems vs Cartridge Systems: The Main Choice
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whole house filters come in a few designs, and the design decides your maintenance more than the brand does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Long-Life Media Tanks</h3>

          <p>
            The Aquasana Rhino and A. O. Smith use a large tank of filter media rated for years: 10 years or 1 million gallons for the Aquasana, 6 years or 600,000 gallons for the A. O. Smith. They need no backflushing and no drain line, so they waste no water. Small pre- and post-filters still need changing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Big Blue Cartridge Systems</h3>

          <p>
            The iSpring, Home Master and Express Water systems use two or three housings in a row, each holding a cartridge. You replace cartridges about once a year, but you can also change the media to suit your water. Standard 20 x 4.5-inch Big Blue cartridges are sold by many brands.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Single-Cartridge Systems</h3>

          <p>
            The 3M Aqua-Pure AP904 and the GE GXWH40L use one housing. They are compact and cheap to install. The 3M uses a quick-change cartridge rated for up to a year; the GE takes a standard GE cartridge changed every three months.
          </p>

