        <div id="source" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. City Water or Well Water? Choose by Your Source
          </h2>

          <p>
            The same filter can be perfect on one supply and wrong on another. Start with where your water comes from.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">City Water: Chlorine or Chloramine</h3>

          <p>
            Municipal water is disinfected, and that disinfectant is what most people taste. Check your utility's annual water quality report, often called a Consumer Confidence Report. If it says chlorine, a carbon system such as the Aquasana handles it well. If it says chloramine, choose catalytic carbon, as in the Home Master HMF2SmgCC.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Well Water: Test First</h3>

          <p>
            A private well has no utility treating or testing it. Iron, manganese, hydrogen sulfide, hardness and bacteria are common, and city water filters are not designed for them. Our guide to the <a href="/best-water-filter-for-well-water/">best water filters for well water</a> covers iron filters, spin-down prefilters and UV. Test the well before you buy anything. If hardness is high, a <a href="/best-water-softener-for-well-water/">water softener for well water</a> needs resin and grain capacity sized for iron, not just calcium.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lead and Other Specific Contaminants</h3>

          <p>
            If you are worried about lead, find out first whether it is in your water and where it comes from; lead often comes from a home's own service line or solder rather than the supply. Then look for a product certified for lead reduction on the specific cartridge, and consider a certified drinking water filter at the kitchen tap as well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flow-rate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Flow Rate and Pipe Size: Will It Lower Your Water Pressure?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water pressure is the worry most people have about whole house filters, and it is mostly a question of matching flow rate and port size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Much Flow You Need</h3>

          <p>
            A shower typically uses about 2 gallons per minute, and a washing machine or dishwasher a few more while filling. A home running two showers and a washing machine at once can need 7 to 10 gallons per minute. The tank systems here are listed at around 14 to 15 GPM, the 3M at up to 20 GPM, and the Home Master at up to 10 GPM.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match the Ports to Your Main Line</h3>

          <p>
            Most homes have a 3/4-inch or 1-inch main. The iSpring, Home Master, 3M and GE use 1-inch ports, the Aquasana kit includes fittings for both sizes, and the A. O. Smith uses 3/4-inch connections. Using a filter with ports smaller than your main line creates a bottleneck, so check before you order.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pressure Drops as Filters Load</h3>

          <p>
            Every filter restricts flow a little more as it catches sediment. Gauges like those on the Express Water system show the drop across the filter. When pressure falls noticeably at the taps, the sediment stage is usually the cause, which is why a pre-filter protects a carbon tank and keeps pressure up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Install a Whole House Water Filter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installation is within reach of a confident DIYer, but it means cutting into the main water line. If that sounds daunting, a plumber can usually fit one in a few hours. These steps follow the general approach the makers describe; follow your system's manual for the details.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Pick the Location</h3>

          <p>
            Install on the main line after the shut-off valve and the water meter, and before the water heater, so hot water is filtered too. Leave room below the housings to remove cartridges, and protect the system from freezing and direct sun.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Shut Off the Water and Drain the Line</h3>

          <p>
            Close the main valve, open a low faucet to drain the pipes, and keep a bucket and towels ready for the water still in the line when you cut it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Fit Valves and a Bypass</h3>

          <p>
            Shut-off valves on each side of the filter, and ideally a bypass loop, let you change filters or service the system without cutting off water to the house. Several kits here include valves or a bypass option.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Keep the Electrical Ground</h3>

          <p>
            In many older homes the electrical system is grounded through metal water pipes. A plastic filter housing can break that path. Install a grounding jumper cable across the filter, and ask an electrician if you are not sure how your home is grounded.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Flush and Check for Leaks</h3>

          <p>
            Turn the water back on slowly, check every joint, and run cold water for the time the manual specifies to flush carbon fines out of new media. Some cloudiness or fine black particles at first are normal with new carbon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Filter Changes and Running Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The price on the box is only part of the cost. Over ten years, filter changes can cost more than the system itself, so compare replacement schedules before you choose.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tank Systems</h3>

          <p>
            The Aquasana's main tank is rated for 10 years, but Aquasana says to change the pre- and post-filters about every two months. The A. O. Smith central filter is rated for six years, and the maker recommends adding a pre-filter, which also needs periodic changes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cartridge Systems</h3>

          <p>
            The iSpring WGB32B is rated at about 100,000 gallons, roughly a year for many homes, across three cartridges. The Home Master carbon filter is rated for 95,000 gallons, about a year for a family of four. The 3M AP904 cartridge is rated for up to a year, and the GE cartridge every three months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Write the Date on the Housing</h3>

          <p>
            Whatever you buy, write the install date on the housing or a tag. Carbon that is past its capacity stops removing chlorine long before it clogs, so falling pressure is not a reliable reminder for carbon stages.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-softener" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Whole House Filter vs Water Softener: Do You Need Both?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is one of the most common questions, because both sit on the main line and both protect appliances. They solve different problems.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Each One Does</h3>

          <p>
            A filter removes sediment and chemicals such as chlorine. A softener uses ion exchange to remove the calcium and magnesium that cause hard water scale. The Aquasana and A. O. Smith listings both say plainly that they are not softeners.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What About Anti-Scale Stages?</h3>

          <p>
            Polyphosphate stages, like the one in the Express Water system, slow scale from forming but do not remove hardness. They help with moderately hard water; very hard water still calls for a softener.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Installing Both</h3>

          <p>
            If you need both, the usual order is sediment filter, then carbon filter, then softener, so chlorine and sediment do not shorten the softener resin's life. See our guide to the <a href="/best-water-softener-system/">best water softener systems</a> for the softener side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drinking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Do You Still Need a Drinking Water Filter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Often, yes. A whole house filter improves every tap, but it is built for flow and capacity, not for the finest contaminant reduction.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When the Whole House Filter Is Enough</h3>

          <p>
            If your only complaint is chlorine taste and sediment on good city water, a whole house carbon system usually fixes drinking water too.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Add an Under-Sink or RO System</h3>

          <p>
            If your water test shows contaminants such as lead, nitrate or PFAS, or you want to reduce total dissolved solids, add a certified point-of-use system at the kitchen sink. Our guide to the <a href="/best-under-sink-water-filter/">best under-sink water filters</a> compares them, and a <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">water filter pitcher</a> is the low-cost option.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Signs Your Home Needs a Whole House Filter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every home needs one. These are the signs that point-of-entry filtration is worth the cost.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You Can Smell Chlorine in the Shower</h3>

          <p>
            A strong pool smell in the bathroom, or dry skin and hair that you associate with your water, are the most common reasons people buy a whole house carbon system.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sediment Clogs Aerators and Appliances</h3>

          <p>
            Grit in faucet aerators, showerheads and appliance inlet screens means sediment is entering the house. A sediment stage at the main, even a simple one like the GE, stops it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stains and Scale</h3>

          <p>
            Reddish stains point to iron, which needs a well water iron filter. White crust on fixtures points to hardness, which needs a softener or at least an anti-scale stage. A water test tells you which you have.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Before Reading Your Water Report</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A city water report tells you whether your supply uses chlorine or chloramine, and that decides the carbon you need. A well needs a lab test. Buying a filter first and testing later is how people end up with a system that does nothing for their actual problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Sediment Pre-Filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Carbon media clogs quickly when it also has to catch sand and rust. A cheap sediment stage in front protects the expensive tank or carbon cartridge and keeps pressure up. A. O. Smith recommends one for its central filter for exactly this reason.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Ports Smaller Than the Main Line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Putting a 3/4-inch filter on a 1-inch main creates a bottleneck that no filter rating can fix. Match the filter's ports to your pipe, or use a system whose kit includes fittings for both sizes, like the Aquasana.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Breaking the Electrical Ground</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Where a home is grounded through metal water pipes, a plastic filter housing interrupts that path. Fit a grounding jumper across the filter and have an electrician check if you are unsure. It is a small step that is easy to forget.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Filter to Soften or Disinfect</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whole house carbon filters do not remove hardness and do not kill bacteria. If your water is hard, add a softener; if a well tests positive for coliform, you need disinfection such as UV. Read each listing's limits before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

