        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best whole house water filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes on city water, the Aquasana Rhino WH-1000-B is the best whole house water filter on Amazon. Its carbon and KDF tank is rated for 1 million gallons or 10 years, it is listed to reduce 97 percent of chlorine, and it is listed at 14.6 GPM. Choose the Home Master instead for chloramine, or a well water system for a private well.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are whole house water filters worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are worth it if you dislike chlorine in your showers, have sediment clogging fixtures and appliances, or want filtered water at every tap without separate filters. They are not worth it if you only want better drinking water, because a pitcher or under-sink filter does that for much less. Check your water report first, then decide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a whole house water filter reduce water pressure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A correctly sized system should not cause a noticeable drop. Choose a filter listed at a flow rate above your home's peak demand, often 7 to 10 GPM, with ports matching your main line. Pressure falls as cartridges load up, so a sudden drop usually means a sediment filter is due for a change.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do you change a whole house water filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the design. Long-life tanks like the Aquasana and A. O. Smith are rated for 10 and 6 years, though their small pre-filters need changing every few months. Cartridge systems like the iSpring and Home Master need new cartridges about once a year, and GE's single cartridge every three months.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a whole house water filter myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many homeowners do, using the included fittings and the maker's instructions. It involves cutting the main line, fitting valves and a bypass, and keeping the electrical ground if your pipes are metal. If you are not comfortable with any of that, a plumber can usually install one in a few hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a whole house water filter soften water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Carbon and sediment filters do not remove the calcium and magnesium that make water hard. Some systems, such as the Express Water Ultimate, add a polyphosphate stage that slows scale, but it does not remove hardness. For hard water you need a water softener, installed after the filter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do whole house water filters work on well water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some do, but well water usually needs different treatment. Carbon systems handle taste and odor, not iron, manganese, hardness or bacteria. Test your well first, then use a system built for its results, such as the iron filters and UV systems in our well water guide.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
